WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:12.920
Hello, everyone, and welcome
to lecture 3 of CME 296.

00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:15.600
So today is a very
special day because we're

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:19.040
going to talk about
flow matching, which

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:24.400
is a generation paradigm that
is quite trendy these days.

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:28.200
So if you're a member,
lectures 1 and lecture 2

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:31.240
were also about
generation paradigm

00:00:31.240 --> 00:00:35.440
where we talked about diffusion
with DPM and lecture 1

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:39.040
and score matching in lecture 2.

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:43.560
So flow matching is yet
another generation paradigm.

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:45.920
And we're going to
see in this lecture

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:49.840
what is the mindset behind
that separate paradigm

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:55.240
and how it relates to
diffusion and score matching.

00:00:55.240 --> 00:01:00.160
So as usual we're going to
start the class with a recap

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:02.760
of the last episodes.

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:07.600
So if you remember
lecture 1, we saw

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:11.040
how we could
generate new samples,

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:13.520
and the way we thought
about doing that

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:18.360
was taking images from
our training set, which

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:21.560
we named clean images.

00:01:21.560 --> 00:01:25.680
And what we did is that we
gradually corrupted them

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:30.560
with a process that we
defined, a process q,

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:34.920
which, given an image
at a given time step,

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:38.720
noised it by adding some
Gaussian noise weighted

00:01:38.720 --> 00:01:41.000
by some coefficients.

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:45.200
And the goal of diffusion
with DPM was to find

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:48.640
or was to learn a
reverse process, which

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:54.120
we noted p theta that
removed the noise gradually.

00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:58.810
And what we saw was that
in order to learn p theta.

00:01:58.810 --> 00:02:05.170
What we did is try to maximize
the likelihood of our model

00:02:05.170 --> 00:02:07.930
seeing the training data.

00:02:07.930 --> 00:02:10.490
And if you remember,
we did some clever math

00:02:10.490 --> 00:02:14.870
with some estimation
of the lower bound.

00:02:14.870 --> 00:02:18.290
So the elbow and we
derived some terms

00:02:18.290 --> 00:02:21.250
and we ended up
with a pretty good

00:02:21.250 --> 00:02:27.810
looking loss, which was just an
L2 regression on the noise that

00:02:27.810 --> 00:02:33.690
was added up to the
image at timestamp t.

00:02:33.690 --> 00:02:37.330
And so this was lecture 1.

00:02:37.330 --> 00:02:42.210
Lecture 2 was taking
another perspective

00:02:42.210 --> 00:02:45.690
and just taking a
step back, thinking

00:02:45.690 --> 00:02:52.410
of images in the space of the
n dimensional space of images,

00:02:52.410 --> 00:02:54.890
and the fact that
what we wanted to do

00:02:54.890 --> 00:02:59.690
was sample from an
easy distribution

00:02:59.690 --> 00:03:06.130
and then work our way up until
the true data distribution.

00:03:06.130 --> 00:03:07.930
So how did we do that?

00:03:07.930 --> 00:03:11.130
The whole point
of the lecture was

00:03:11.130 --> 00:03:14.930
to learn what we call
the score, which is

00:03:14.930 --> 00:03:18.770
the gradient of the log of p.

00:03:18.770 --> 00:03:20.730
And why did we do that?

00:03:20.730 --> 00:03:24.410
Because it acted as
a compass from where

00:03:24.410 --> 00:03:29.650
we were up until
regions of high density.

00:03:29.650 --> 00:03:33.610
And if we learn such a
score, what we could do

00:03:33.610 --> 00:03:39.210
was use sampling techniques
such as Langevin sampling, which

00:03:39.210 --> 00:03:44.210
allowed us to move towards
high density regions,

00:03:44.210 --> 00:03:50.810
but also allow for some
diversity with some noise term.

00:03:50.810 --> 00:03:56.140
And we saw that in order for
us to estimate that score,

00:03:56.140 --> 00:04:01.940
we cleverly noised the initial
data distribution in order

00:04:01.940 --> 00:04:06.780
to learn the score of
the noised distribution.

00:04:06.780 --> 00:04:12.620
And then we had a
progressive noise schedule

00:04:12.620 --> 00:04:16.300
that allowed us to
progressively work our way up

00:04:16.300 --> 00:04:18.120
until the data distribution.

00:04:18.120 --> 00:04:22.380
So we ended up with
a score function

00:04:22.380 --> 00:04:25.760
that was a function of
the position in space,

00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:28.420
but also the noise level.

00:04:28.420 --> 00:04:31.320
And again, after doing
some clever math,

00:04:31.320 --> 00:04:35.380
we also ended up with an
L2 regression loss, which

00:04:35.380 --> 00:04:38.580
was quite easy to compute.

00:04:38.580 --> 00:04:42.060
But what we also saw was
that what we saw in lecture 2

00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:45.700
was actually quite related
to what we saw in lecture 1,

00:04:45.700 --> 00:04:50.580
because both involved
adding noise.

00:04:50.580 --> 00:04:53.020
And in particular,
we saw that there

00:04:53.020 --> 00:04:59.180
was a relationship between the
score of a noisy distribution

00:04:59.180 --> 00:05:04.220
and the noise that we
added from the clean image

00:05:04.220 --> 00:05:07.460
to the noisy image.

00:05:07.460 --> 00:05:11.700
So given that, what
we did was think

00:05:11.700 --> 00:05:16.580
of a unified way of looking
at both these perspectives,

00:05:16.580 --> 00:05:20.740
and we moved from a
discrete representation

00:05:20.740 --> 00:05:24.980
to a continuous one, and
we obtained this SDE, which

00:05:24.980 --> 00:05:31.260
was of the form dx equal f
of x and t dt plus gt times

00:05:31.260 --> 00:05:36.140
dw, where w is our Wiener
process which is introducing

00:05:36.140 --> 00:05:38.700
the stochasticity.

00:05:38.700 --> 00:05:42.780
And in particular, the first
term is called the drift term,

00:05:42.780 --> 00:05:47.060
and it is a term that quantifies
the deterministic movement

00:05:47.060 --> 00:05:49.780
in space across time.

00:05:49.780 --> 00:05:53.260
And the second term is
called the diffusion term,

00:05:53.260 --> 00:05:57.340
which quantifies the
amount of stochasticity

00:05:57.340 --> 00:06:00.500
that you have in your process.

00:06:00.500 --> 00:06:04.900
So this expression is the
expression of the forward pass,

00:06:04.900 --> 00:06:09.900
meaning, you're actually
noising your images.

00:06:09.900 --> 00:06:13.060
And we saw that in
space, if we were

00:06:13.060 --> 00:06:15.740
to represent that,
it meant, again,

00:06:15.740 --> 00:06:21.860
having a drift component
and a diffusion component.

00:06:21.860 --> 00:06:25.220
So now if we wanted
to denoise our image,

00:06:25.220 --> 00:06:29.380
we were actually interested
in the reverse process,

00:06:29.380 --> 00:06:35.660
which we saw thanks to the
result from the '80s involved

00:06:35.660 --> 00:06:37.420
knowing the score.

00:06:37.420 --> 00:06:41.340
So this expression is
the reverse process

00:06:41.340 --> 00:06:43.900
that allows us to
denoise images, which is

00:06:43.900 --> 00:06:46.860
why we need to learn the score.

00:06:46.860 --> 00:06:50.310
And this is where
the score comes in,

00:06:50.310 --> 00:06:51.770
in the continuous view.

00:06:54.390 --> 00:06:58.550
So this was two weeks
ago and a week ago.

00:06:58.550 --> 00:07:03.770
But today we're going to see
this third generation paradigm,

00:07:03.770 --> 00:07:06.470
which is called flow matching.

00:07:06.470 --> 00:07:09.590
And similar to the
past lectures, what

00:07:09.590 --> 00:07:11.710
we're going to try
to do is to have

00:07:11.710 --> 00:07:17.270
a nice balance between
intuition and math,

00:07:17.270 --> 00:07:20.250
because you will see that
these papers are full of math,

00:07:20.250 --> 00:07:22.790
full of equations,
and we will try

00:07:22.790 --> 00:07:25.010
to approach it from an
intuitive perspective,

00:07:25.010 --> 00:07:29.110
but not shy away
from the math either.

00:07:29.110 --> 00:07:31.670
And of course, please
interrupt me at any point

00:07:31.670 --> 00:07:37.670
if you feel there's
something that's not clear.

00:07:37.670 --> 00:07:39.390
Cool?

00:07:39.390 --> 00:07:41.870
OK, let's start.

00:07:41.870 --> 00:07:46.670
So the whole point of
these first three lectures

00:07:46.670 --> 00:07:51.110
is to find a way to
go from somewhere

00:07:51.110 --> 00:07:56.430
that is easy to sample
from up until our data

00:07:56.430 --> 00:07:59.670
distribution of interests.

00:07:59.670 --> 00:08:03.550
So the way that flow
matching works is.

00:08:03.550 --> 00:08:06.590
It has two distributions.

00:08:06.590 --> 00:08:09.510
The first one is called
the initial distribution,

00:08:09.510 --> 00:08:13.382
which is the distribution
that you're starting from.

00:08:13.382 --> 00:08:15.590
And then the second distribution
is called the target

00:08:15.590 --> 00:08:18.270
distribution, and it
is the distribution

00:08:18.270 --> 00:08:21.510
that you want to end up with.

00:08:21.510 --> 00:08:25.590
And the whole point
of flow matching

00:08:25.590 --> 00:08:33.510
is finding a way to transport
our initial data distribution up

00:08:33.510 --> 00:08:36.390
until the target distribution.

00:08:36.390 --> 00:08:41.350
And just note the terminology
I'm using, transport.

00:08:41.350 --> 00:08:47.850
So what we want is to transport
the whole density that we have,

00:08:47.850 --> 00:08:50.630
and remember that
probability, densities

00:08:50.630 --> 00:08:53.750
have this very nice
property of summing to 1.

00:08:53.750 --> 00:08:58.030
So we want to transport the
whole density distribution

00:08:58.030 --> 00:09:02.310
from one place to another.

00:09:02.310 --> 00:09:05.870
So this is the idea
behind flow matching.

00:09:05.870 --> 00:09:09.830
And we're going to see
how we can do that.

00:09:09.830 --> 00:09:16.870
So I'm going to just give you a
warning in terms of conventions.

00:09:16.870 --> 00:09:21.790
In the past, we've seen
that 0 was clean data

00:09:21.790 --> 00:09:24.730
and then capital T
was the noise data.

00:09:24.730 --> 00:09:27.590
But here it's going
to be different.

00:09:27.590 --> 00:09:32.790
So we will be noting p0 our
initial data distribution, which

00:09:32.790 --> 00:09:35.150
is typically something
that is easy to sample

00:09:35.150 --> 00:09:38.630
from such as Gaussian noise.

00:09:38.630 --> 00:09:41.470
And the target data
distribution is

00:09:41.470 --> 00:09:46.560
going to be noted p1,
t equal 1, and this is

00:09:46.560 --> 00:09:50.240
where we want to end up with.

00:09:50.240 --> 00:09:52.200
And then again, so
this slide is just

00:09:52.200 --> 00:09:56.640
to say that lectures 1
and 2 we said 0 was clean,

00:09:56.640 --> 00:09:59.840
capital T was noise.

00:09:59.840 --> 00:10:05.460
But here 0 will be noise
and 1 will be what we want,

00:10:05.460 --> 00:10:10.360
which is the clean images,
which is the data distribution.

00:10:10.360 --> 00:10:14.480
So the reason why we
chose to proceed that way

00:10:14.480 --> 00:10:18.120
is just that the field
chose these conventions.

00:10:18.120 --> 00:10:22.360
And if we were to keep the
conventions of the diffusion

00:10:22.360 --> 00:10:26.480
and score matching fields,
it would just completely

00:10:26.480 --> 00:10:30.200
confuse if you were to read
papers from the flow matching

00:10:30.200 --> 00:10:34.400
field, which is the reason
why we're keeping things

00:10:34.400 --> 00:10:37.320
consistent with the paper.

00:10:37.320 --> 00:10:39.300
But just keep those
things in mind,

00:10:39.300 --> 00:10:42.240
because sometimes these
conventions can also

00:10:42.240 --> 00:10:45.180
be reversed, depending on
which paper you're reading.

00:10:45.180 --> 00:10:50.040
But this one is by
far the most common.

00:10:50.040 --> 00:10:55.320
So with that warning
aside, what we want to do

00:10:55.320 --> 00:11:01.760
is to sample an observation from
our initial data distribution

00:11:01.760 --> 00:11:07.040
and then map it to the
target data distribution.

00:11:07.040 --> 00:11:09.440
So in order to do
that, we're now

00:11:09.440 --> 00:11:12.520
going to go through
some common notations.

00:11:12.520 --> 00:11:16.560
And the first one is
called the trajectory.

00:11:16.560 --> 00:11:21.080
So the trajectory is
notated xt, and it

00:11:21.080 --> 00:11:27.040
is the path that is taken by
an observation between times 0

00:11:27.040 --> 00:11:28.440
and 1.

00:11:28.440 --> 00:11:31.760
So by the way, the time
0 and 1 is arbitrary.

00:11:31.760 --> 00:11:35.200
It's just a way for us to
say, OK, when are we starting

00:11:35.200 --> 00:11:37.960
and when are we ending?

00:11:37.960 --> 00:11:41.670
And so here at time t
equals 0, we have x0.

00:11:41.670 --> 00:11:44.730
And what we want to do
is for our trajectory

00:11:44.730 --> 00:11:52.730
to map a point from
p0 up until p1.

00:11:52.730 --> 00:11:55.250
So that's our goal.

00:11:55.250 --> 00:11:58.150
So now, let's see
another notation.

00:11:58.150 --> 00:12:01.010
So this one is called the flow.

00:12:01.010 --> 00:12:08.210
The flow is known
as psi of T of x0.

00:12:08.210 --> 00:12:11.050
So I'm just going to
write that down here.

00:12:11.050 --> 00:12:23.770
So it is a function that takes
time and an initial condition.

00:12:23.770 --> 00:12:30.850
And it gives you where
that point at time t.

00:12:30.850 --> 00:12:34.830
So this is the time
so belongs to 01.

00:12:34.830 --> 00:12:39.410
This one is to your
space, which is maybe--

00:12:39.410 --> 00:12:41.490
I don't know-- d dimensional.

00:12:41.490 --> 00:12:45.110
And this one is also in
the d dimensional space.

00:12:47.890 --> 00:12:50.530
And you have psi
of T of x0, which

00:12:50.530 --> 00:12:57.370
is equal to your point at
time t, which is x of t.

00:12:57.370 --> 00:13:01.330
So the flow you can interpret
it in different ways.

00:13:01.330 --> 00:13:04.730
So one way you can
interpret it is it's

00:13:04.730 --> 00:13:07.330
a collection of trajectories.

00:13:07.330 --> 00:13:11.290
Because if you change
the initial condition x0,

00:13:11.290 --> 00:13:15.970
then psi t of x0 is all
the possible trajectories

00:13:15.970 --> 00:13:24.230
that go from an initial
condition x0 up until time t.

00:13:24.230 --> 00:13:24.730
Yeah?

00:13:27.410 --> 00:13:30.570
The flow can also be
seen as a function that

00:13:30.570 --> 00:13:37.570
maps your initial condition to
where it should be at time t.

00:13:37.570 --> 00:13:40.890
And this is why it's
called the flow.

00:13:40.890 --> 00:13:43.512
So this function
is quite important

00:13:43.512 --> 00:13:44.970
because at the end
of the day, what

00:13:44.970 --> 00:13:53.450
you want is psi 1 of x0 to be
sampled from-- to be as if it

00:13:53.450 --> 00:13:56.890
was sampled from p1, which is
your target distribution, data

00:13:56.890 --> 00:13:57.710
distribution.

00:14:00.690 --> 00:14:01.190
Cool.

00:14:01.190 --> 00:14:03.970
So now we have our
next terminology

00:14:03.970 --> 00:14:10.930
which is called the probability
path, and it's denoted pt of x.

00:14:10.930 --> 00:14:15.490
So pt of x is the
probability distribution

00:14:15.490 --> 00:14:20.050
of your observations at time t.

00:14:20.050 --> 00:14:26.730
So p0 of x is your initial
data distribution, and p1 of x

00:14:26.730 --> 00:14:30.530
is your target
data distribution.

00:14:30.530 --> 00:14:34.900
So if t is between 0
and 1, it's basically

00:14:34.900 --> 00:14:41.500
the intermediary probability
data distribution.

00:14:41.500 --> 00:14:47.340
And as you can imagine, in
order to go from p0 to p1,

00:14:47.340 --> 00:14:51.260
you have an infinite
number of ways

00:14:51.260 --> 00:14:54.300
of going from one to the other.

00:14:54.300 --> 00:14:58.980
So pt by no means unique.

00:14:58.980 --> 00:15:02.480
There are many ways for
you to go from 0 to 1.

00:15:05.740 --> 00:15:06.260
Cool.

00:15:06.260 --> 00:15:11.380
And now we're going to
talk about a central object

00:15:11.380 --> 00:15:16.660
of this whole field, and
it's called the vector field.

00:15:16.660 --> 00:15:21.300
So vector field
is noted ut of x.

00:15:21.300 --> 00:15:27.980
And it is a function that takes
in a place in space, so x,

00:15:27.980 --> 00:15:30.100
and a time t.

00:15:30.100 --> 00:15:33.540
And it gives you a vector.

00:15:33.540 --> 00:15:36.260
And that vector,
you can interpret it

00:15:36.260 --> 00:15:41.820
as the direction and the speed
that particles should move

00:15:41.820 --> 00:15:44.120
at that location at that time.

00:15:47.060 --> 00:15:50.240
So again, the vector
field takes in a vector,

00:15:50.240 --> 00:15:52.380
which is your position
in space and gives you

00:15:52.380 --> 00:15:58.140
also a vector, which is where
you need to move and how fast.

00:15:58.140 --> 00:16:02.660
And we're going to see
that the vector field is

00:16:02.660 --> 00:16:06.900
quite central to flow matching.

00:16:06.900 --> 00:16:11.320
But before we do that,
I know last lecture,

00:16:11.320 --> 00:16:13.620
we talked about the score.

00:16:13.620 --> 00:16:17.540
And the score was also a vector.

00:16:17.540 --> 00:16:20.500
And if you remember,
the score was also

00:16:20.500 --> 00:16:26.940
something we used in order
to go from noise to data.

00:16:26.940 --> 00:16:29.900
So one natural question you may
be asking yourself right now

00:16:29.900 --> 00:16:36.700
is, well, how does this vector
field or velocity relate

00:16:36.700 --> 00:16:38.700
to the score?

00:16:38.700 --> 00:16:41.780
So I'm going to give you
the following analogy.

00:16:41.780 --> 00:16:45.980
So let's suppose you want to go
from your initial distribution

00:16:45.980 --> 00:16:48.060
to your target distribution.

00:16:48.060 --> 00:16:50.020
And let's suppose
that you're thinking

00:16:50.020 --> 00:16:55.640
of this in terms of self-driving
cars that are distributed,

00:16:55.640 --> 00:17:00.100
let's say, in a Gaussian fashion
in the middle of the desert.

00:17:00.100 --> 00:17:06.300
And what you want to do is to
position your self-driving cars

00:17:06.300 --> 00:17:09.520
in a certain way so that
it follows, let's say,

00:17:09.520 --> 00:17:12.940
a target data distribution.

00:17:12.940 --> 00:17:16.900
So the way you would
think of the velocity

00:17:16.900 --> 00:17:19.500
would be instructions
that you would

00:17:19.500 --> 00:17:24.619
give to your self-driving
cars at a given location

00:17:24.619 --> 00:17:30.950
and at a given time t So if
the vector field was not time

00:17:30.950 --> 00:17:34.430
dependent, you could even think
of it as highways or routes

00:17:34.430 --> 00:17:35.830
with speed limits.

00:17:35.830 --> 00:17:38.430
But here, the vector
field is actually

00:17:38.430 --> 00:17:43.670
time dependent, which means that
at a given location x and at two

00:17:43.670 --> 00:17:48.590
different time steps, t1 and
t2, that vector can actually

00:17:48.590 --> 00:17:51.070
be different.

00:17:51.070 --> 00:17:54.110
And in that sense,
just following

00:17:54.110 --> 00:17:57.910
with that self-driving
car example,

00:17:57.910 --> 00:18:02.310
the score would be
a bit like a compass

00:18:02.310 --> 00:18:06.750
that you could see in your
car of where high density

00:18:06.750 --> 00:18:09.630
regions are.

00:18:09.630 --> 00:18:12.150
So I hope this is
useful to just picture

00:18:12.150 --> 00:18:16.450
the difference between what a
velocity is and what a score is.

00:18:16.450 --> 00:18:18.550
So these two are
vectors, but they

00:18:18.550 --> 00:18:20.450
have different interpretations.

00:18:24.270 --> 00:18:25.450
Any questions so far?

00:18:28.230 --> 00:18:32.775
We've barely started, so we're
going to see a few things here.

00:18:32.775 --> 00:18:35.150
But I just want to make sure
that the notation so far are

00:18:35.150 --> 00:18:35.690
clear.

00:18:38.870 --> 00:18:41.230
Yeah, all good?

00:18:41.230 --> 00:18:41.850
Cool.

00:18:41.850 --> 00:18:46.790
So we're going to see
our first identity here.

00:18:46.790 --> 00:18:52.310
So we introduced the vector
field, a.k.a. the velocity,

00:18:52.310 --> 00:18:58.870
because this is the way we're
going to have our particles

00:18:58.870 --> 00:19:01.990
move from one place to another.

00:19:01.990 --> 00:19:04.550
And in particular,
the definition

00:19:04.550 --> 00:19:07.830
we're giving to the velocity,
a.k.a. the vector field,

00:19:07.830 --> 00:19:10.030
is given as follows.

00:19:10.030 --> 00:19:15.630
So what we're going to say
is that a change in position

00:19:15.630 --> 00:19:19.790
will be associated with the
velocity at that location

00:19:19.790 --> 00:19:24.440
at that time t times dt.

00:19:24.440 --> 00:19:29.440
So in other words, a given
particle in a given location

00:19:29.440 --> 00:19:33.860
will be moving according
to that velocity.

00:19:37.400 --> 00:19:40.600
And one thing that
I want to tell you

00:19:40.600 --> 00:19:44.300
is that the trajectory,
which as a reminder,

00:19:44.300 --> 00:19:48.800
is the whole path that the
particle is taking from time t

00:19:48.800 --> 00:19:53.880
equals 0 to time t equal to
1, that trajectory is actually

00:19:53.880 --> 00:19:59.600
going to be unique for a
given initial condition

00:19:59.600 --> 00:20:02.600
if there is a special condition
on the vector field, which

00:20:02.600 --> 00:20:07.500
is that if the vector field
is Lipschitz continuous.

00:20:10.400 --> 00:20:12.280
So the reason why
I'm bringing this up

00:20:12.280 --> 00:20:14.920
is because it's going
to be important.

00:20:14.920 --> 00:20:18.680
And so who knows what
Lipschitz continuity is?

00:20:18.680 --> 00:20:21.480
Have you ever
heard of this term?

00:20:21.480 --> 00:20:23.280
Yeah?

00:20:23.280 --> 00:20:24.000
No?

00:20:24.000 --> 00:20:27.120
OK, so maybe I can just recap
the mathematical definition

00:20:27.120 --> 00:20:31.640
and maybe how it
means-- what it means.

00:20:31.640 --> 00:20:35.560
So Lipschitz continuity
of a function f

00:20:35.560 --> 00:20:41.160
is when you have some
constants, let's call

00:20:41.160 --> 00:20:50.040
it m such that for all x and
y in your space of interest,

00:20:50.040 --> 00:21:01.900
you have your function f that
satisfies this relationship.

00:21:01.900 --> 00:21:05.740
So the norm of f of x
minus f of y is less than

00:21:05.740 --> 00:21:11.320
or equal than m times
the norm of x minus y.

00:21:11.320 --> 00:21:17.320
So what this means is that f
needs to be continuous, but also

00:21:17.320 --> 00:21:20.380
not in a very dramatic way.

00:21:23.160 --> 00:21:24.800
So this is the
mathematical definition

00:21:24.800 --> 00:21:25.980
of Lipschitz continuity.

00:21:25.980 --> 00:21:30.160
And what I'm saying here
is if the vector field

00:21:30.160 --> 00:21:37.480
is Lipschitz continuous,
then the trajectories x

00:21:37.480 --> 00:21:41.660
will be unique for a
given initial condition.

00:21:43.985 --> 00:21:45.360
And so I'm going
to just give you

00:21:45.360 --> 00:21:51.020
an example of when
trajectories are not unique.

00:21:55.960 --> 00:22:04.800
So here we have our ODE
which is here like so.

00:22:04.800 --> 00:22:09.560
So I'm just going to give you an
example of if we have a vector

00:22:09.560 --> 00:22:13.200
field that is not Lipschitz
continuous, how we can have

00:22:13.200 --> 00:22:15.600
trajectories that start
from the same point

00:22:15.600 --> 00:22:18.330
but that are actually different.

00:22:18.330 --> 00:22:20.350
So here, the example
is as follows.

00:22:20.350 --> 00:22:23.690
So let's suppose
we have your vector

00:22:23.690 --> 00:22:30.950
field, which is given this, by,
let's say, square root of x.

00:22:30.950 --> 00:22:36.650
And you only consider, by
the way, x positive or 0.

00:22:36.650 --> 00:22:39.570
So if your vector field, by
the way, is time independent

00:22:39.570 --> 00:22:47.650
and it's just a function of
x by square root of x, then

00:22:47.650 --> 00:22:51.850
if you say you want your
initial condition to be x0

00:22:51.850 --> 00:23:05.210
is equal to 0, then xt equal
to 0 is going to be a solution,

00:23:05.210 --> 00:23:13.330
but xt equals to
t squared over 4

00:23:13.330 --> 00:23:16.730
is also going to be a solution.

00:23:16.730 --> 00:23:18.750
So here t squared over 4.

00:23:18.750 --> 00:23:22.610
So the derivative is t over 2.

00:23:22.610 --> 00:23:26.610
And here's when you put this
also in the square root,

00:23:26.610 --> 00:23:30.050
it's also t over 2.

00:23:30.050 --> 00:23:35.330
So all of that to say that
your vector field needs

00:23:35.330 --> 00:23:38.010
to behave in a
certain way if you

00:23:38.010 --> 00:23:41.810
want to guarantee that your
trajectories are unique.

00:23:41.810 --> 00:23:46.330
And this is
Lipschitz continuity.

00:23:46.330 --> 00:23:48.750
And you're going to
see why that matters.

00:23:51.690 --> 00:23:52.190
Cool.

00:23:52.190 --> 00:23:54.910
So that's the first equation
that's going to be important.

00:23:54.910 --> 00:23:59.610
So the ODE here,
which is dx over dt

00:23:59.610 --> 00:24:03.790
is equal to the velocity
at location x and time t.

00:24:03.790 --> 00:24:05.770
It's the first one.

00:24:05.770 --> 00:24:10.010
The second one that we're
going to see is the following.

00:24:10.010 --> 00:24:13.570
So I told you that
what we want to do

00:24:13.570 --> 00:24:21.250
is transport our distribution
from one place to another.

00:24:21.250 --> 00:24:24.170
So when you transport
something, one thing

00:24:24.170 --> 00:24:26.690
that you want to guarantee
is that you're not

00:24:26.690 --> 00:24:32.770
going to lose or gain anything
of what you're transporting.

00:24:32.770 --> 00:24:37.530
You have what we call
conservation of mass.

00:24:37.530 --> 00:24:42.730
And this idea is also something
we want to put in writing.

00:24:42.730 --> 00:24:48.170
And in particular, we're going
to focus on the following case.

00:24:48.170 --> 00:24:51.010
So let's assume you're
in a region of space

00:24:51.010 --> 00:24:55.650
and you consider
a very small area.

00:24:55.650 --> 00:25:00.890
And here you have your data
distribution that is around it.

00:25:00.890 --> 00:25:04.050
So the equation that we
want to put in writing

00:25:04.050 --> 00:25:11.730
is around making sure that
the evolution of your density

00:25:11.730 --> 00:25:17.300
at time t is equal
to the density that

00:25:17.300 --> 00:25:26.260
is coming minus the density that
is going out or that is leaving.

00:25:26.260 --> 00:25:28.580
Do you all agree with this idea?

00:25:28.580 --> 00:25:32.780
So the evolution of
density as at time t

00:25:32.780 --> 00:25:34.900
is a function of how
much density comes

00:25:34.900 --> 00:25:41.020
in minus how many comes out.

00:25:41.020 --> 00:25:44.940
So the left-hand
side is kind of easy.

00:25:44.940 --> 00:25:49.820
It's just a partial derivative
of your probability path

00:25:49.820 --> 00:25:54.900
at that region with
respect to time.

00:25:54.900 --> 00:26:00.420
But then the right side, I don't
about you, but very intuitively,

00:26:00.420 --> 00:26:07.340
there's not like a supernatural
operator that does that.

00:26:07.340 --> 00:26:10.260
So what we're
going to see now is

00:26:10.260 --> 00:26:14.220
how we can quantify the
idea on the right side

00:26:14.220 --> 00:26:16.580
of the equal sign.

00:26:16.580 --> 00:26:19.780
But before we do that, I
just want to take a pause.

00:26:19.780 --> 00:26:24.380
Does this idea make sense,
conservation of mass?

00:26:24.380 --> 00:26:29.620
By the way, have you
seen it in another topic?

00:26:29.620 --> 00:26:31.460
Yeah?

00:26:31.460 --> 00:26:33.900
So in physics, you
have the same thing,

00:26:33.900 --> 00:26:35.720
and it's actually
called-- it has a name.

00:26:35.720 --> 00:26:39.500
It's called the
continuity equation.

00:26:39.500 --> 00:26:43.260
Anyways, so instead of just
giving you the formula,

00:26:43.260 --> 00:26:47.940
we're just going to
intuitively get to it.

00:26:47.940 --> 00:26:52.500
And in particular, I want us to
think about this right-hand side

00:26:52.500 --> 00:26:55.220
of this equation
and think for a bit

00:26:55.220 --> 00:27:02.180
how we want to quantify the
inflow minus outflow bit.

00:27:02.180 --> 00:27:07.260
So let's suppose you
have an operator that's

00:27:07.260 --> 00:27:10.070
called of the divergence.

00:27:10.070 --> 00:27:13.430
But right now, I'm not
telling you anything

00:27:13.430 --> 00:27:15.130
about what that operator is.

00:27:15.130 --> 00:27:20.590
I'm just telling you that
the operator is positive

00:27:20.590 --> 00:27:24.530
when things are coming
out more than coming in.

00:27:27.510 --> 00:27:31.150
And we're going to
see what happens then.

00:27:31.150 --> 00:27:37.130
So here, when we're talking
about coming in and coming out,

00:27:37.130 --> 00:27:41.470
we're, of course,
talking about vectors.

00:27:41.470 --> 00:27:45.010
So here we're
assuming, for instance,

00:27:45.010 --> 00:27:47.710
if there's more coming
out than coming in that--

00:27:47.710 --> 00:27:53.670
let's assume if we are
in one dimensional space,

00:27:53.670 --> 00:28:00.510
you have a vector that is
such that at first it's

00:28:00.510 --> 00:28:06.430
small in magnitude, and then
it's much bigger in magnitude.

00:28:06.430 --> 00:28:08.830
So here, do you agree
with me that whatever

00:28:08.830 --> 00:28:11.430
this vector is
representing, there is more

00:28:11.430 --> 00:28:14.790
coming out than coming in?

00:28:14.790 --> 00:28:16.190
You all agree?

00:28:16.190 --> 00:28:19.765
OK, so that's one
case where coming out

00:28:19.765 --> 00:28:20.890
can be more than coming in.

00:28:20.890 --> 00:28:22.450
So let's see a second case.

00:28:22.450 --> 00:28:25.370
So second case is here.

00:28:25.370 --> 00:28:28.830
And then let's say your vector
is coming out here and coming

00:28:28.830 --> 00:28:31.470
out there.

00:28:31.470 --> 00:28:33.950
Yeah?

00:28:33.950 --> 00:28:35.770
I'm sure you can have
more cases than that.

00:28:35.770 --> 00:28:43.430
But in this 1D example, if you
were to represent your vector,

00:28:43.430 --> 00:28:52.480
let's say f, over the
dimension x, here,

00:28:52.480 --> 00:28:56.750
what is the
magnitude of f doing?

00:28:56.750 --> 00:28:58.670
It is increasing.

00:28:58.670 --> 00:29:04.990
OK, so here, we just see that
partial derivative of x/x

00:29:04.990 --> 00:29:06.030
is increasing.

00:29:06.030 --> 00:29:06.990
How about here?

00:29:06.990 --> 00:29:08.090
So here it's negative.

00:29:08.090 --> 00:29:10.230
And then it becomes positive.

00:29:10.230 --> 00:29:11.490
Also increasing, right?

00:29:16.870 --> 00:29:24.270
OK, so based on this very
back-of-the-envelope intuition,

00:29:24.270 --> 00:29:28.990
we see that when
things are leaving,

00:29:28.990 --> 00:29:34.510
partial derivative of x over
this dimension of f over this

00:29:34.510 --> 00:29:40.670
dimension x is positive, which
seems to match the sign that we

00:29:40.670 --> 00:29:45.790
want from our
divergence operation.

00:29:45.790 --> 00:29:48.290
So now let's see the other case.

00:29:48.290 --> 00:29:52.270
So let's assume that we have
now the case where we want

00:29:52.270 --> 00:29:57.110
our operator to be negative
when there is, quote unquote,

00:29:57.110 --> 00:30:00.990
"more coming than leaving."

00:30:00.990 --> 00:30:05.220
So here again, this example.

00:30:05.220 --> 00:30:08.760
So you have, let's say, a
bigger vector here coming

00:30:08.760 --> 00:30:12.960
and then a smaller one leaving.

00:30:12.960 --> 00:30:15.640
And then let's say you
have another case, which

00:30:15.640 --> 00:30:20.760
is given by this, which
is just vectors pointing

00:30:20.760 --> 00:30:22.740
towards that area.

00:30:25.280 --> 00:30:30.920
So here you see that f is
becoming more and more negative

00:30:30.920 --> 00:30:33.320
when you project it on x.

00:30:33.320 --> 00:30:36.920
So here you have
partial derivative

00:30:36.920 --> 00:30:40.500
of f over x, which is negative.

00:30:40.500 --> 00:30:44.920
And here you see that
it was first positive,

00:30:44.920 --> 00:30:52.800
and then it becomes negative,
which again, boils down to this.

00:30:52.800 --> 00:30:57.360
So this is by no
means a formal proof,

00:30:57.360 --> 00:31:04.560
but I hope I gave you some
intuition that if you take

00:31:04.560 --> 00:31:09.840
the partial derivative of your
function that is outputting

00:31:09.840 --> 00:31:15.560
a vector over its
coordinates, at least in 1D,

00:31:15.560 --> 00:31:19.017
it would give you this
intuition that if it's positive,

00:31:19.017 --> 00:31:21.100
then it's more coming out,
and when it's negative,

00:31:21.100 --> 00:31:24.600
then there's more coming in.

00:31:24.600 --> 00:31:28.800
So based on this intuition,
what I'm now telling

00:31:28.800 --> 00:31:32.400
you is that there is an
operator that is indeed

00:31:32.400 --> 00:31:38.360
called the divergence that is
a function of a vector, that

00:31:38.360 --> 00:31:44.080
is a function of x, which
is also multidimensional.

00:31:44.080 --> 00:31:48.680
And what it is
doing is it is just

00:31:48.680 --> 00:31:52.640
the sum of the partial
derivatives of x

00:31:52.640 --> 00:31:56.520
over each dimension.

00:31:56.520 --> 00:32:00.440
So the divergence is a scalar.

00:32:00.440 --> 00:32:02.283
It's a number.

00:32:02.283 --> 00:32:04.200
And if it's positive,
it just means that there

00:32:04.200 --> 00:32:05.325
are more things coming out.

00:32:05.325 --> 00:32:10.080
And if it's negative, it means
there are more things coming in.

00:32:10.080 --> 00:32:16.960
So based on that, we want to see
how we can use this divergence

00:32:16.960 --> 00:32:21.920
operator back into our
continuity equation example,

00:32:21.920 --> 00:32:28.880
where we want to quantify
the fact that we want

00:32:28.880 --> 00:32:34.080
the net inflows, which
is how much density

00:32:34.080 --> 00:32:38.200
is coming in versus out.

00:32:38.200 --> 00:32:40.640
So now a second
question is something

00:32:40.640 --> 00:32:44.960
that we ask ourselves, which
is OK, what should we use as f?

00:32:44.960 --> 00:32:47.120
What should f be?

00:32:47.120 --> 00:32:48.800
So we saw the
vector field, which

00:32:48.800 --> 00:32:52.240
is a vector that
could be a candidate.

00:32:52.240 --> 00:32:54.740
So do you think the vector
field could be a good candidate?

00:32:58.810 --> 00:33:01.270
So let's see with an example.

00:33:01.270 --> 00:33:04.210
So let's suppose you
have two points in space,

00:33:04.210 --> 00:33:05.630
let's say x1 and x2.

00:33:05.630 --> 00:33:10.410
And then you have their
respective vector fields.

00:33:10.410 --> 00:33:14.650
If you happen to have a higher
density in one of the two

00:33:14.650 --> 00:33:18.450
points, then our
intuition tells us

00:33:18.450 --> 00:33:22.850
that you would want the point
in the higher density region

00:33:22.850 --> 00:33:27.370
to transport more things than
the one in the lower density

00:33:27.370 --> 00:33:30.330
region.

00:33:30.330 --> 00:33:34.690
So in that case, the vector
field alone would actually not

00:33:34.690 --> 00:33:37.650
be a good candidate.

00:33:37.650 --> 00:33:41.090
Instead, and maybe you
have guessed it, in order

00:33:41.090 --> 00:33:46.230
to quantify the movement
of density, we, of course,

00:33:46.230 --> 00:33:50.970
need to take into consideration
the probability density.

00:33:50.970 --> 00:33:53.930
And so here the actual
quantity that we will use

00:33:53.930 --> 00:33:57.770
is called the
probability flux, and it

00:33:57.770 --> 00:34:04.050
is defined by dt,
which is a scalar times

00:34:04.050 --> 00:34:06.390
ut, which is the vector field.

00:34:10.210 --> 00:34:14.969
So going back to
the equation that we

00:34:14.969 --> 00:34:18.449
wanted to put in writing,
what we're saying

00:34:18.449 --> 00:34:24.449
is that the temporal evolution
of the density at time t, which

00:34:24.449 --> 00:34:29.170
we said is simply the partial
derivative of p with respect

00:34:29.170 --> 00:34:30.810
to t.

00:34:30.810 --> 00:34:36.530
We're now saying that this is
equal to minus the divergence

00:34:36.530 --> 00:34:39.770
of the probability flux.

00:34:39.770 --> 00:34:44.850
And this quantity, so minus
divergence of dt ut of x.

00:34:44.850 --> 00:34:50.889
It just means that we're
interested in the net inflows

00:34:50.889 --> 00:34:57.060
of density within
our small volume.

00:34:57.060 --> 00:35:03.860
So all in all, that equation,
which I'm going to write,

00:35:03.860 --> 00:35:08.780
is nothing else than the
continuity equation, which

00:35:08.780 --> 00:35:10.360
you may have seen in physics.

00:35:16.860 --> 00:35:19.320
So I'm going to write it down.

00:35:19.320 --> 00:35:21.240
And of course, OK, this is here.

00:35:31.100 --> 00:35:36.180
So what we're saying is that the
evolution of density at a given

00:35:36.180 --> 00:35:40.820
location, the temporal evolution
of the density at a given

00:35:40.820 --> 00:35:46.380
location, is given by how much
density is coming in minus

00:35:46.380 --> 00:35:48.860
how much density is coming out.

00:35:48.860 --> 00:35:57.300
And this just conveys
the facts that we're not

00:35:57.300 --> 00:35:59.740
creating new density
out of thin air,

00:35:59.740 --> 00:36:08.260
we're just conserving it
when time goes from t0 to 1.

00:36:08.260 --> 00:36:14.460
And compared to the
ODE that we saw before,

00:36:14.460 --> 00:36:17.620
you can think of this
equation as being

00:36:17.620 --> 00:36:24.260
a macro perspective of how
your probability path evolves.

00:36:24.260 --> 00:36:28.340
And the ODE itself
is actually centered

00:36:28.340 --> 00:36:31.380
on how one particle
evolves, which

00:36:31.380 --> 00:36:35.680
is more a micro perspective,
which we're going to see here.

00:36:35.680 --> 00:36:40.140
So a single sample, you take
your sample x at time t.

00:36:40.140 --> 00:36:45.220
And what you're saying is that
how fast that particle is going

00:36:45.220 --> 00:36:48.180
is actually given
by the vector field

00:36:48.180 --> 00:36:51.500
at that location at that time.

00:36:51.500 --> 00:36:59.020
So the distance dx is
given by ut of x times dt.

00:36:59.020 --> 00:37:02.700
So this is the
micro perspective.

00:37:02.700 --> 00:37:06.020
But we also saw that
this vector field that

00:37:06.020 --> 00:37:10.100
was coming into the picture
in this micro perspective

00:37:10.100 --> 00:37:12.900
is also in the
macro perspective.

00:37:12.900 --> 00:37:16.660
And what we're saying is
given that we're transporting

00:37:16.660 --> 00:37:19.820
the density and we're not
losing it or gaining it,

00:37:19.820 --> 00:37:24.380
we also have this nice
identity that we can use,

00:37:24.380 --> 00:37:26.920
which is also called
the continuity equation.

00:37:30.340 --> 00:37:36.260
And so there is expression that
you will see a lot in papers.

00:37:36.260 --> 00:37:38.420
I'm going to read it out loud.

00:37:38.420 --> 00:37:44.460
The vector field ut of x
generates the probability path.

00:37:44.460 --> 00:37:47.060
So when people say
that, what they mean

00:37:47.060 --> 00:37:55.910
is that ut and dt satisfy
the continuity equation.

00:37:55.910 --> 00:38:00.470
So now you may wonder,
well, why should we care?

00:38:00.470 --> 00:38:04.550
So just remember
that our goal is

00:38:04.550 --> 00:38:09.870
to sample an observation from
the initial distribution p0,

00:38:09.870 --> 00:38:15.110
and to map it to the
target data distribution.

00:38:15.110 --> 00:38:18.270
And the way we want
to map it is we

00:38:18.270 --> 00:38:21.550
want that particle
to go from one place

00:38:21.550 --> 00:38:25.150
to another using
the vector field.

00:38:25.150 --> 00:38:30.870
And so what we want is to find
a probability path that will

00:38:30.870 --> 00:38:35.590
allow us to go from p0 to p1.

00:38:35.590 --> 00:38:38.570
And in order to find
the probability path,

00:38:38.570 --> 00:38:43.070
we also need to find the
associated vector field that

00:38:43.070 --> 00:38:46.590
will allow us to actually do
the mapping because that vector

00:38:46.590 --> 00:38:54.390
field is in the single
sample perspective.

00:38:54.390 --> 00:38:55.490
So that's the idea.

00:38:55.490 --> 00:38:58.090
We're going to see it in
other words, in a little bit.

00:38:58.090 --> 00:39:03.790
But this is the intuition
behind flow models.

00:39:03.790 --> 00:39:07.430
Any questions on this?

00:39:07.430 --> 00:39:10.490
Everything is clear so far?

00:39:10.490 --> 00:39:10.990
Yep.

00:39:14.710 --> 00:39:17.030
So question is the
solution of this equation

00:39:17.030 --> 00:39:17.890
would be the target.

00:39:17.890 --> 00:39:25.390
So on the left-hand
side, if we know ut

00:39:25.390 --> 00:39:31.070
and if we know that ut is
allowing pt to go from p0,

00:39:31.070 --> 00:39:33.630
something that we decide
that is easy to sample

00:39:33.630 --> 00:39:38.070
to p1, what we care
about, what we're saying

00:39:38.070 --> 00:39:45.360
is if we know such a ut,
then on the left-hand side,

00:39:45.360 --> 00:39:51.520
if you sample x from p0, you
have your initial condition.

00:39:51.520 --> 00:39:54.640
And if you ut,
what that means is

00:39:54.640 --> 00:40:00.680
you can solve this equation
in order to find x1.

00:40:00.680 --> 00:40:07.440
And x1, by definition, will be
as if it was sample from p1.

00:40:07.440 --> 00:40:08.540
So that's the idea.

00:40:11.200 --> 00:40:12.500
I know it was a lot.

00:40:12.500 --> 00:40:15.260
I know it may not seem
clear, but don't worry,

00:40:15.260 --> 00:40:19.200
we have a lot of
examples down the line.

00:40:19.200 --> 00:40:20.100
So let's move on.

00:40:20.100 --> 00:40:22.820
Oh, this is exactly my
next slide actually.

00:40:22.820 --> 00:40:26.680
So here what we're saying is the
relationship between these two

00:40:26.680 --> 00:40:28.360
perspectives, I'm
going to just repeat

00:40:28.360 --> 00:40:30.040
what I said but in
mathematical terms,

00:40:30.040 --> 00:40:37.480
is if we sample x0 from p0.

00:40:37.480 --> 00:40:45.040
Given that our particle
x is subject to velocity,

00:40:45.040 --> 00:40:49.240
then what that means is, if
that velocity is actually

00:40:49.240 --> 00:40:54.480
generating the probability path
from the initial to the target

00:40:54.480 --> 00:41:02.280
one, then what that means is
that xt will be sampled from dt.

00:41:02.280 --> 00:41:06.220
And in particular, t=1, x1
will be sampled from p1.

00:41:11.160 --> 00:41:11.860
OK, cool.

00:41:11.860 --> 00:41:12.780
So don't worry.

00:41:12.780 --> 00:41:15.960
We're going to go into
more details on this.

00:41:15.960 --> 00:41:25.400
So the whole goal of flow models
is to map points, is to map x0

00:41:25.400 --> 00:41:27.920
to x1.

00:41:27.920 --> 00:41:30.100
So what is our strategy here?

00:41:30.100 --> 00:41:34.240
We saw that the vector
field was a central piece

00:41:34.240 --> 00:41:36.160
of this whole problem.

00:41:36.160 --> 00:41:39.240
So number one,
step number one is

00:41:39.240 --> 00:41:46.600
for us to estimate this
vector field via some model

00:41:46.600 --> 00:41:50.200
that we're going to
note ut theta of x.

00:41:50.200 --> 00:41:54.240
So theta is the parameters
that we're learning.

00:41:54.240 --> 00:41:57.440
So if we learn such
a vector field,

00:41:57.440 --> 00:42:02.200
then what we're going to
do in order to map x0 to x1

00:42:02.200 --> 00:42:09.040
is we're going to use the
ODE and solve it numerically.

00:42:09.040 --> 00:42:10.400
So what does that mean?

00:42:10.400 --> 00:42:14.360
That means that we're
going to sample x0,

00:42:14.360 --> 00:42:17.960
and we're just going to
follow the vector field up

00:42:17.960 --> 00:42:20.920
until time equal to 1.

00:42:20.920 --> 00:42:22.760
So this integral
here in practice

00:42:22.760 --> 00:42:27.200
is going to be just a numerical
solver that maybe Euler,

00:42:27.200 --> 00:42:29.560
for instance.

00:42:29.560 --> 00:42:33.780
And it's going to be giving
you x1, which is what you want,

00:42:33.780 --> 00:42:36.760
because at the end of the day,
you want to be able to sample

00:42:36.760 --> 00:42:40.450
from your target
data distribution.

00:42:40.450 --> 00:42:45.730
So starting from
now, our full focus

00:42:45.730 --> 00:42:52.410
will be around finding the
vector field ut theta of x.

00:42:52.410 --> 00:42:55.190
Because right now, I just
told you what we care about.

00:42:55.190 --> 00:42:57.930
But I didn't tell you how
we're going to do that.

00:42:57.930 --> 00:43:02.250
So now we're going
to focus on that.

00:43:02.250 --> 00:43:06.530
So in the past, there
has been many papers

00:43:06.530 --> 00:43:12.770
published on how you can
estimate your vector field.

00:43:12.770 --> 00:43:17.350
And there is one method that
I'm going to mention here,

00:43:17.350 --> 00:43:19.650
which is not going to be
the focus of this class

00:43:19.650 --> 00:43:24.590
because it is a method that
was used a number of years ago.

00:43:24.590 --> 00:43:26.570
So eight years ago, I believe.

00:43:26.570 --> 00:43:29.710
But it is not something that
could be used in practice.

00:43:29.710 --> 00:43:31.190
So people are not
using it so far.

00:43:31.190 --> 00:43:36.610
But I'm just telling you
just for historical purposes.

00:43:36.610 --> 00:43:41.290
So one way of learning
the vector fields

00:43:41.290 --> 00:43:47.970
is by maximizing the likelihood
that your model is seeing

00:43:47.970 --> 00:43:50.370
the data, the training set.

00:43:50.370 --> 00:43:51.810
So how can you do that?

00:43:51.810 --> 00:43:55.770
Well, if you take the
continuity equation that we saw,

00:43:55.770 --> 00:43:58.810
so this one--

00:43:58.810 --> 00:44:03.930
so if you take this
equation, then what happens

00:44:03.930 --> 00:44:07.890
is if you do some
math, you can actually

00:44:07.890 --> 00:44:11.530
pop up that log
of dt of x, which

00:44:11.530 --> 00:44:15.410
is what you want to maximize.

00:44:15.410 --> 00:44:19.750
And you can have the
following strategy.

00:44:19.750 --> 00:44:26.670
So you want to determine
log of p1 of x1.

00:44:26.670 --> 00:44:29.830
So x1 is the data
from the training set.

00:44:29.830 --> 00:44:34.530
So you want to compute the
probability of your model

00:44:34.530 --> 00:44:36.410
seeing x1.

00:44:36.410 --> 00:44:40.850
The problem is you
don't how to compute it.

00:44:40.850 --> 00:44:44.250
So the way you're going
to do this is you're

00:44:44.250 --> 00:44:49.170
first going to realize that
you can compute the probability

00:44:49.170 --> 00:44:54.370
that the initial distribution
is seeing x0, because typically,

00:44:54.370 --> 00:44:57.170
the initial distribution is an
easy one, something that you

00:44:57.170 --> 00:45:00.150
can analytically compute,
such as a Gaussian.

00:45:00.150 --> 00:45:04.410
So you know how to
compute log p0 of x0.

00:45:04.410 --> 00:45:06.170
And what you're
going to do is you're

00:45:06.170 --> 00:45:11.170
going to link x1 and
x0 by integrating

00:45:11.170 --> 00:45:16.010
the divergence of the vector
fields, because you have--

00:45:16.010 --> 00:45:18.530
the formula above that is true.

00:45:18.530 --> 00:45:23.950
So if you know log p0 of x0 and
if you know the vector field,

00:45:23.950 --> 00:45:26.290
then you can compute
this quantity.

00:45:26.290 --> 00:45:27.870
And so what that
paper was doing,

00:45:27.870 --> 00:45:31.410
which is called continuous
normalizing flow,

00:45:31.410 --> 00:45:36.060
was that it was us trying to
learn the parameters theta

00:45:36.060 --> 00:45:43.060
by maximizing the likelihood
of your training sets

00:45:43.060 --> 00:45:45.140
happening under theta.

00:45:45.140 --> 00:45:49.580
And it was computing the
likelihood using this formula.

00:45:49.580 --> 00:45:51.740
So what is the problem?

00:45:51.740 --> 00:45:55.140
The problem is that
at training time,

00:45:55.140 --> 00:45:59.040
you need to constantly
solve this integral.

00:45:59.040 --> 00:46:03.620
So it's a numerical solver
that works all the time.

00:46:03.620 --> 00:46:06.460
So it's very expensive
and very slow,

00:46:06.460 --> 00:46:09.900
which is why we
have a snail emoji.

00:46:09.900 --> 00:46:11.540
So it's just not practical.

00:46:11.540 --> 00:46:16.340
People just couldn't use that.

00:46:16.340 --> 00:46:20.540
And for that
reason, I hope I did

00:46:20.540 --> 00:46:23.620
a good job of motivating
the reason why

00:46:23.620 --> 00:46:25.140
we needed another approach.

00:46:25.140 --> 00:46:27.820
The approach we're
going to see now

00:46:27.820 --> 00:46:32.100
is actually what is called
flow matching, because we're

00:46:32.100 --> 00:46:35.960
going to see we're not doing
maximum likelihood estimation.

00:46:35.960 --> 00:46:43.940
We're instead trying to
directly learn the vector field.

00:46:43.940 --> 00:46:46.020
So if you remember
for score matching,

00:46:46.020 --> 00:46:48.820
you were directly trying
to learn the score.

00:46:48.820 --> 00:46:53.400
So here we're directly trying
to learn the vector field.

00:46:53.400 --> 00:46:59.100
But the problem is that
you don't know what it is.

00:46:59.100 --> 00:47:05.500
So we're going to see how
we can compute this loss

00:47:05.500 --> 00:47:12.860
and indeed optimize on theta in
order to find our vector field.

00:47:12.860 --> 00:47:18.460
So just remember that the
problem that we have is we

00:47:18.460 --> 00:47:21.060
have an initial data
distribution and a target data

00:47:21.060 --> 00:47:22.860
distribution.

00:47:22.860 --> 00:47:26.340
And what we care
about is finding

00:47:26.340 --> 00:47:32.150
this ut of x, which will
allow us to go from p0 to p1.

00:47:32.150 --> 00:47:34.390
That is our goal.

00:47:34.390 --> 00:47:38.310
Well, p0 is easy, so it's
a Gaussian typically.

00:47:38.310 --> 00:47:43.030
But then p1 can be
super complicated.

00:47:43.030 --> 00:47:48.390
So one idea we can
have is what if we just

00:47:48.390 --> 00:47:51.870
remember that the target
data distribution is just

00:47:51.870 --> 00:47:55.290
composed of points from
your training data?

00:47:55.290 --> 00:47:58.310
So it's like individual points.

00:47:58.310 --> 00:48:03.150
So what if instead we
simplify our problem

00:48:03.150 --> 00:48:07.870
and we ask ourselves how we
can transport the initial data

00:48:07.870 --> 00:48:14.950
distribution, not to the target
one, but to a single point?

00:48:14.950 --> 00:48:18.790
How about if we just move
our whole data distribution

00:48:18.790 --> 00:48:21.090
from initial to a single point?

00:48:21.090 --> 00:48:24.310
And here, I noted
the single point

00:48:24.310 --> 00:48:28.150
with the distribution delta x1.

00:48:28.150 --> 00:48:33.370
So delta x1 is the notation
for a Dirac delta distribution.

00:48:33.370 --> 00:48:36.110
So who has heard of
the Dirac distribution?

00:48:36.110 --> 00:48:37.870
Yeah?

00:48:37.870 --> 00:48:40.132
So Dirac distribution
is actually probably

00:48:40.132 --> 00:48:41.590
the simplest
distribution out there

00:48:41.590 --> 00:48:44.470
because it's a
deterministic distribution.

00:48:44.470 --> 00:48:47.990
It will always give
you one quantity.

00:48:47.990 --> 00:48:53.670
So here delta-- so I'm
going to write it down.

00:48:53.670 --> 00:49:06.230
So delta x1 of x is going to
be 0 if x is different than x1.

00:49:06.230 --> 00:49:08.530
And otherwise, it will be
actually a very high number.

00:49:08.530 --> 00:49:13.710
So typically, people
say, let's say infinity.

00:49:13.710 --> 00:49:16.590
But of course, it's
infinity, but the integral

00:49:16.590 --> 00:49:21.350
of this distribution, like
the density needs to be 1.

00:49:21.350 --> 00:49:27.710
So long story short is that
we simplified our problem

00:49:27.710 --> 00:49:33.670
from initial data distribution
to your target one.

00:49:33.670 --> 00:49:37.670
So we changed from that
formulation to initial data

00:49:37.670 --> 00:49:41.390
distribution to a single point.

00:49:41.390 --> 00:49:49.750
So one idea could be for us
to think of the following way

00:49:49.750 --> 00:49:51.090
of going from one to the other.

00:49:51.090 --> 00:49:54.110
So let's suppose you
have a probability

00:49:54.110 --> 00:49:57.450
path that is super simple.

00:49:57.450 --> 00:50:00.630
It's just a Gaussian
distribution

00:50:00.630 --> 00:50:09.750
that is being interpolated
between 0 and x1.

00:50:09.750 --> 00:50:16.470
So here what I'm saying is,
how about we think of the way

00:50:16.470 --> 00:50:20.030
we want to go from
0 to the points

00:50:20.030 --> 00:50:25.760
by taking our normal
distribution of mean 0

00:50:25.760 --> 00:50:29.300
and variance identity?

00:50:29.300 --> 00:50:31.660
And how about we
just scale it down,

00:50:31.660 --> 00:50:34.800
scale it down and then
move it towards x1

00:50:34.800 --> 00:50:39.040
until it becomes
just that point?

00:50:39.040 --> 00:50:43.840
So here pt-- so we say
x given x1, because it's

00:50:43.840 --> 00:50:48.920
a conditional probability, it's
like given we're arriving at x1

00:50:48.920 --> 00:50:56.520
at time t equal 1, it will
be given by a mean of t x1

00:50:56.520 --> 00:51:02.440
and a variance of 1 minus
t squared times identity.

00:51:02.440 --> 00:51:06.260
So let me tell you
why that is the case.

00:51:15.480 --> 00:51:23.840
So what I'm saying here is
that I'm proposing a way for us

00:51:23.840 --> 00:51:29.840
to go from the normal
distribution that

00:51:29.840 --> 00:51:34.820
is this one of mean 0 and
then variance the identity.

00:51:34.820 --> 00:51:38.840
So what I'm saying is in order
to go from here to this point,

00:51:38.840 --> 00:51:42.760
which is x1, what
I'm telling you

00:51:42.760 --> 00:51:50.520
is we're going to have a
path pt given x1, which

00:51:50.520 --> 00:51:54.000
is a normal distribution of--

00:51:54.000 --> 00:51:59.335
as a function of t because it's
probability path at time t,

00:51:59.335 --> 00:52:03.880
t x1 and 1 minus t squared I.

00:52:03.880 --> 00:52:12.240
So when t equals 0, this
is 0, this is identity.

00:52:12.240 --> 00:52:16.200
So p0 is just the
normal distribution of 0

00:52:16.200 --> 00:52:21.160
and I. And p1 is--

00:52:21.160 --> 00:52:25.680
well, t=1 is just x1.

00:52:25.680 --> 00:52:28.500
And this one is a variance of 0.

00:52:28.500 --> 00:52:31.640
So you can think of
t just tending to 0.

00:52:31.640 --> 00:52:37.280
So p1 is just a
Dirac distribution.

00:52:37.280 --> 00:52:43.280
So this probability path
that I'm telling you

00:52:43.280 --> 00:52:49.760
is something that is letting
us go from our initial data

00:52:49.760 --> 00:52:51.020
distribution to this point.

00:52:51.020 --> 00:52:53.040
So it's a valid one.

00:52:53.040 --> 00:52:55.960
So why am I choosing this?

00:52:55.960 --> 00:53:01.440
Well it can be shown that
one vector field that

00:53:01.440 --> 00:53:03.960
is generating this
probability path

00:53:03.960 --> 00:53:05.500
has the following expression.

00:53:05.500 --> 00:53:12.960
So ut of x given x1 is x1
minus x over 1 minus t.

00:53:12.960 --> 00:53:16.520
So I'm just giving you the
formula so you can derive it.

00:53:16.520 --> 00:53:19.770
So I'm looking at the time,
we will not have time.

00:53:19.770 --> 00:53:24.170
But the reason why I proposed
the probability path,

00:53:24.170 --> 00:53:25.770
the conditional
probability path is

00:53:25.770 --> 00:53:31.510
because it's much easier for
us to compute a vector field,

00:53:31.510 --> 00:53:35.490
and it's something that
we can actually write.

00:53:35.490 --> 00:53:36.930
And it is this one.

00:53:36.930 --> 00:53:40.050
And what I'm going
to tell you is

00:53:40.050 --> 00:53:43.850
that this vector
field is generating

00:53:43.850 --> 00:53:47.490
the conditional
probability path.

00:53:47.490 --> 00:53:50.010
So in other words,
the vector field

00:53:50.010 --> 00:53:54.690
will be such that if
you sample from p0

00:53:54.690 --> 00:53:57.770
of this conditional
probability path,

00:53:57.770 --> 00:54:02.170
and if you follow these
conditional vector fields,

00:54:02.170 --> 00:54:04.930
what I am telling you
is that you will end up

00:54:04.930 --> 00:54:10.010
at time equal t
with xt following

00:54:10.010 --> 00:54:14.330
your conditional
probability path.

00:54:14.330 --> 00:54:18.570
Well, that's a huge
step forward, no?

00:54:18.570 --> 00:54:20.270
So how do you prove
that, by the way?

00:54:20.270 --> 00:54:26.090
So you just use the quantities,
you use the vector field that

00:54:26.090 --> 00:54:29.970
is just this formula, and you
use the conditional probability

00:54:29.970 --> 00:54:32.510
paths which you can
analytically derive.

00:54:32.510 --> 00:54:35.750
Because, if you remember
the Gaussian distribution,

00:54:35.750 --> 00:54:37.870
we just know
everything about it.

00:54:37.870 --> 00:54:42.570
We also know the
density formula.

00:54:42.570 --> 00:54:51.930
So if you take both formula
and if you use the continuity

00:54:51.930 --> 00:54:56.770
equation, which is
here, you can show

00:54:56.770 --> 00:55:00.610
that this expression is true.

00:55:00.610 --> 00:55:02.210
And this is how you prove that.

00:55:02.210 --> 00:55:06.930
Now we're not going to do it
because this is just some math.

00:55:06.930 --> 00:55:08.690
But maybe one thing
I will tell you

00:55:08.690 --> 00:55:14.240
is why that vector
fields makes sense.

00:55:18.380 --> 00:55:20.320
So we said that--

00:55:24.860 --> 00:55:29.540
we said that the conditional
vector field, which

00:55:29.540 --> 00:55:33.980
is noted ut of x
given x1, what we said

00:55:33.980 --> 00:55:41.180
was that this was equal to
x1 minus x over 1 minus t.

00:55:41.180 --> 00:55:51.700
Well, when t is approaching 1,
and when your x is not near x1,

00:55:51.700 --> 00:55:55.080
then what you're seeing is
that this quantity goes crazy,

00:55:55.080 --> 00:55:56.720
goes very close to infinity.

00:55:56.720 --> 00:55:59.140
It goes very, very high.

00:55:59.140 --> 00:56:01.580
Well, why does that make sense?

00:56:01.580 --> 00:56:05.940
It's because at time t equal 1,
your distribution is literally

00:56:05.940 --> 00:56:10.380
a deterministic
distribution that is x1.

00:56:10.380 --> 00:56:15.300
So you want all your
particles to be exactly at x1.

00:56:15.300 --> 00:56:21.460
So if you're not at x1,
then you better hurry.

00:56:21.460 --> 00:56:25.460
Your velocity will
be very, very high.

00:56:25.460 --> 00:56:27.420
So that's one thing
I want to say.

00:56:27.420 --> 00:56:29.980
And one other
thing I want to say

00:56:29.980 --> 00:56:32.860
is there is a very
nice expression

00:56:32.860 --> 00:56:40.940
of the conditional
vector fields if xt

00:56:40.940 --> 00:56:46.140
is drawn from that conditional
probability path, which

00:56:46.140 --> 00:56:51.500
as a reminder, is a normal
distribution of mean t x1

00:56:51.500 --> 00:56:55.500
