So what I'm telling you is by
definition of the expectation--

01:17:09.700 --> 01:17:14.780
so this is just a
bunch of integrals.

01:17:14.780 --> 01:17:22.300
So this is the integral over
t over x1 and x of ut of theta

01:17:22.300 --> 01:17:27.460
of x dot product with the
conditional vector field

01:17:27.460 --> 01:17:30.660
ut of x given x1.

01:17:30.660 --> 01:17:37.920
And this is over x1 and x so I'm
going to say it's p data of x1.

01:17:37.920 --> 01:17:40.980
And then the conditional
probability path x

01:17:40.980 --> 01:17:48.540
given x1 dx1, dx, dt.

01:17:48.540 --> 01:17:50.700
So far so good?

01:17:50.700 --> 01:17:55.540
So here what we
notice is that if we

01:17:55.540 --> 01:18:04.380
put this quantity here,
this quantity here,

01:18:04.380 --> 01:18:18.500
and if I divide by dt of x and I
do times dt of x, then what I'm

01:18:18.500 --> 01:18:23.500
going to recognize is
the marginal vector field

01:18:23.500 --> 01:18:26.980
that we have constructed.

01:18:26.980 --> 01:18:33.050
So this is going to be equal to
the integral over t integral.

01:18:33.050 --> 01:18:36.610
So this integral over x1
is also going to come here.

01:18:40.270 --> 01:18:52.830
So integral over t, integral
over x of ut theta of x.

01:18:52.830 --> 01:18:57.990
And this ut of x, which is
the marginal vector field,

01:18:57.990 --> 01:19:05.570
times this marginal probability
path dt of x, dx, dt.

01:19:08.270 --> 01:19:13.910
And this is nothing else
than the expectation over t

01:19:13.910 --> 01:19:18.070
and x, which is run from
the marginal probability

01:19:18.070 --> 01:19:24.710
path of the dot product
of ut theta of x

01:19:24.710 --> 01:19:32.950
and the marginal vector field,
which is exactly what we want.

01:19:32.950 --> 01:19:37.990
So what I'm showing you here is
that the marginal vector field,

01:19:37.990 --> 01:19:41.030
the way we
constructed it, allows

01:19:41.030 --> 01:19:44.790
us to optimize on a loss,
the conditional flow matching

01:19:44.790 --> 01:19:48.390
loss, which is much
more tractable,

01:19:48.390 --> 01:19:52.650
and leads us to the same
gradients with respect to theta.

01:19:56.270 --> 01:20:01.850
And so long story short,
we have this loss,

01:20:01.850 --> 01:20:05.950
which is called the conditional
flow matching loss, which

01:20:05.950 --> 01:20:11.790
is the expectation over t x1,
which is run over the pdata,

01:20:11.790 --> 01:20:14.670
and then x drawn over the
conditional probability

01:20:14.670 --> 01:20:19.990
path of a quantity that
is extremely simple.

01:20:19.990 --> 01:20:21.710
So it's just the
squared distance

01:20:21.710 --> 01:20:27.430
between the learned velocity,
which is what you want to learn,

01:20:27.430 --> 01:20:32.430
and the conditional
vector field,

01:20:32.430 --> 01:20:37.230
which we saw was nothing
else than just x1 minus x0.

01:20:37.230 --> 01:20:40.230
So it's a very
tractable loss, and it

01:20:40.230 --> 01:20:44.470
does exactly what you want.

01:20:44.470 --> 01:20:45.330
Any questions?

01:20:45.330 --> 01:20:46.130
So it's a lot.

01:20:53.150 --> 01:20:54.970
Are we all clear with this?

01:20:58.550 --> 01:21:03.410
OK, I'm going to say that it's
OK if you feel like it's a lot.

01:21:03.410 --> 01:21:04.410
It is indeed a lot.

01:21:04.410 --> 01:21:07.670
So I'm going to
recap the strategy.

01:21:07.670 --> 01:21:11.230
So what we said at
the very beginning

01:21:11.230 --> 01:21:14.870
was that we had
two different views

01:21:14.870 --> 01:21:18.230
of how we want to go
from one distribution

01:21:18.230 --> 01:21:21.110
to another distribution.

01:21:21.110 --> 01:21:25.040
What we said was that there was
this quantity called the vector

01:21:25.040 --> 01:21:31.200
field, a.k.a. the velocity that
was linked to how we could move

01:21:31.200 --> 01:21:36.560
our particles, so with the ODE
dx over dt equal vector field

01:21:36.560 --> 01:21:39.440
that one, but also
the continuity

01:21:39.440 --> 01:21:45.720
equation that linked the vector
field with the probability path.

01:21:45.720 --> 01:21:49.600
So what we want is to find
the vector field that will

01:21:49.600 --> 01:21:53.880
allow us to go from p0 to p1.

01:21:53.880 --> 01:21:57.040
So what you need to
do is to find first

01:21:57.040 --> 01:22:00.680
a probability path that goes
from p1 to-- sorry, from p0

01:22:00.680 --> 01:22:02.440
to p1.

01:22:02.440 --> 01:22:04.520
First you need to find that.

01:22:04.520 --> 01:22:09.680
And then you need to construct
a vector field that indeed

01:22:09.680 --> 01:22:13.080
allows you to go from p0 to p1.

01:22:13.080 --> 01:22:15.680
So how do you do--
how do you do that?

01:22:15.680 --> 01:22:20.080
Well, you propose
some quantities

01:22:20.080 --> 01:22:24.520
and you try to see if the
continuity equation is indeed

01:22:24.520 --> 01:22:27.600
satisfied.

01:22:27.600 --> 01:22:30.160
So that's step number one.

01:22:30.160 --> 01:22:35.080
Once you your vector
field of interest,

01:22:35.080 --> 01:22:37.680
your goal now is to learn it.

01:22:37.680 --> 01:22:40.640
Because if you learn
it, then what you can do

01:22:40.640 --> 01:22:43.320
is to integrate the ODE.

01:22:43.320 --> 01:22:44.940
Why do you want to
integrate the ODE?

01:22:44.940 --> 01:22:47.460
It's because you want
to go from x0 to x1.

01:22:50.000 --> 01:22:54.880
Therefore, you need to
learn your vector field.

01:22:54.880 --> 01:23:01.920
And here what we did is, in
order to learn the vector field,

01:23:01.920 --> 01:23:07.040
we first thought of
a very simple case

01:23:07.040 --> 01:23:11.000
of us wanting to
go from the initial

01:23:11.000 --> 01:23:15.280
to a single point via the
conditional probability

01:23:15.280 --> 01:23:19.040
path and the conditional
vector field.

01:23:19.040 --> 01:23:22.690
So this is the simple case
with the Dirac distribution

01:23:22.690 --> 01:23:25.810
being the target distribution.

01:23:25.810 --> 01:23:30.170
And then what we said was OK,
given this very simple case that

01:23:30.170 --> 01:23:36.010
is super tractable, let's try to
match our initial distribution

01:23:36.010 --> 01:23:39.690
with the actual target
one of interest, which

01:23:39.690 --> 01:23:42.350
is the whole data distribution,
not just a single point,

01:23:42.350 --> 01:23:43.830
but the whole data distribution.

01:23:46.370 --> 01:23:50.130
And we proposed the
marginal probability

01:23:50.130 --> 01:23:57.730
path which satisfies t equals
0 equals p0 and t equal 1.

01:23:57.730 --> 01:23:59.070
It's equal to p data.

01:23:59.070 --> 01:24:02.770
So it was a valid
probability path.

01:24:02.770 --> 01:24:06.730
And we also saw that the
constructed vector field

01:24:06.730 --> 01:24:10.850
was actually generating that
proposed marginal probability

01:24:10.850 --> 01:24:11.350
path.

01:24:11.350 --> 01:24:13.790
It was generating it, why?

01:24:13.790 --> 01:24:19.410
Because it satisfies the
continuity equation, which

01:24:19.410 --> 01:24:21.510
I said I don't have
time to derive,

01:24:21.510 --> 01:24:25.810
but you just need to
replace the quantities

01:24:25.810 --> 01:24:32.530
and see that the continuity
equation is indeed satisfied.

01:24:32.530 --> 01:24:37.070
So given that we were finally
with the vector field,

01:24:37.070 --> 01:24:42.250
that was something that was
adapted to our use case.

01:24:42.250 --> 01:24:44.730
But now what we wanted
to do was to learn it

01:24:44.730 --> 01:24:47.690
with the tractable loss.

01:24:47.690 --> 01:24:51.290
So in this last
part, what we did

01:24:51.290 --> 01:24:58.210
is show that the loss
that we wanted to optimize

01:24:58.210 --> 01:25:01.050
was actually equivalent
to another loss that

01:25:01.050 --> 01:25:04.610
was extremely tractable, which
is the conditional flow matching

01:25:04.610 --> 01:25:07.490
loss.

01:25:07.490 --> 01:25:10.930
And the way we showed
that was by showing

01:25:10.930 --> 01:25:13.330
that the gradient of this
losses with respect to theta

01:25:13.330 --> 01:25:15.950
were equal, which
is what I did here.

01:25:19.410 --> 01:25:22.270
And then finally, we have
our final loss function,

01:25:22.270 --> 01:25:32.290
which is ut theta of x minus x1
minus x0 expectation of that.

01:25:32.290 --> 01:25:34.730
So it's incredibly simple.

01:25:34.730 --> 01:25:39.850
So everything I told you is
actually-- so a lot of it

01:25:39.850 --> 01:25:42.770
is coming from this paper
that's called Flow Matching

01:25:42.770 --> 01:25:45.090
for Generative Modeling.

01:25:45.090 --> 01:25:48.450
So I highly
recommend reading it.

01:25:48.450 --> 01:25:51.430
It may not be the
simplest to read,

01:25:51.430 --> 01:25:55.970
but I hope what I
just told you actually

01:25:55.970 --> 01:26:00.810
will allow you to understand the
formulas in there a bit better.

01:26:00.810 --> 01:26:04.430
But I didn't want to
give it to Shervine

01:26:04.430 --> 01:26:07.770
before telling you a bit
more about why we're calling

01:26:07.770 --> 01:26:10.450
this whole thing flow matching.

01:26:10.450 --> 01:26:12.770
Because core matching,
it was called core

01:26:12.770 --> 01:26:16.500
matching because you
tried to match the score.

01:26:16.500 --> 01:26:19.360
But here, we're not
matching the flow,

01:26:19.360 --> 01:26:20.680
we're matching the velocity.

01:26:20.680 --> 01:26:23.980
So one natural question
is, why is it not

01:26:23.980 --> 01:26:25.200
called velocity matching?

01:26:25.200 --> 01:26:26.992
Why is it not called
vector field matching?

01:26:26.992 --> 01:26:29.860
Why is it called flow matching?

01:26:29.860 --> 01:26:32.200
So these points are
a bit more informal.

01:26:32.200 --> 01:26:36.460
It's just a personal take
on why that is the case.

01:26:36.460 --> 01:26:41.220
So one potential reason
is, historically,

01:26:41.220 --> 01:26:43.920
these models which
are flow models,

01:26:43.920 --> 01:26:49.340
meaning trying to map a point
to another via this psi t of x

01:26:49.340 --> 01:26:53.440
minus 0, they had the
term flow in their names,

01:26:53.440 --> 01:26:58.020
and in particular the
method that I mentioned

01:26:58.020 --> 01:27:03.180
that try to learn the vector
field with maximum likelihood

01:27:03.180 --> 01:27:03.960
estimation.

01:27:03.960 --> 01:27:07.780
That one was called
continuous normalizing flows.

01:27:07.780 --> 01:27:13.700
So one reason is maybe
branding, but actually, I

01:27:13.700 --> 01:27:19.380
want to get back to my earlier
Lipschitz continuity comment.

01:27:19.380 --> 01:27:25.460
It turns out that if the vector
field that you learn here

01:27:25.460 --> 01:27:31.260
is Lipschitz continuous,
then what happens

01:27:31.260 --> 01:27:34.120
is that your trajectories,
they're all unique.

01:27:36.820 --> 01:27:41.940
So you actually have a
mapping between vector fields

01:27:41.940 --> 01:27:46.740
that you learn and the flow.

01:27:46.740 --> 01:27:48.420
So if you're matching
the velocity,

01:27:48.420 --> 01:27:52.060
you're also matching the flow.

01:27:52.060 --> 01:27:54.460
Now you may be
wondering, OK, how

01:27:54.460 --> 01:28:00.380
do that the learned vector field
is indeed Lipschitz continuous?

01:28:00.380 --> 01:28:02.600
Well, we have not seen that yet.

01:28:02.600 --> 01:28:07.660
We're going to see
that in lecture 5.

01:28:07.660 --> 01:28:11.660
But the model that
is behind this

01:28:11.660 --> 01:28:14.420
learned vector
field is something

01:28:14.420 --> 01:28:17.140
that is composed of
matrix multiplications

01:28:17.140 --> 01:28:21.260
and some smooth
activation functions.

01:28:21.260 --> 01:28:26.820
All of that is
Lipschitz continuous.

01:28:26.820 --> 01:28:28.780
So what you will
learn is actually

01:28:28.780 --> 01:28:31.660
Lipschitz continuous
vector field

01:28:31.660 --> 01:28:35.940
with respect to
x, which is going

01:28:35.940 --> 01:28:41.900
to give you a one-to-one mapping
between velocity and flow.

01:28:41.900 --> 01:28:44.840
And we have two minutes.

01:28:44.840 --> 01:28:46.440
So bear with me.

01:28:49.420 --> 01:28:51.100
In two minutes,
I'm going to cover

01:28:51.100 --> 01:28:54.020
how we train and do inference.

01:28:54.020 --> 01:28:56.940
But it's going to be very easy.

01:28:56.940 --> 01:28:59.620
So let's see how we do training.

01:28:59.620 --> 01:29:04.980
So as I mentioned, the
loss is extremely simple.

01:29:04.980 --> 01:29:08.160
And so what we do is
we sample the noise.

01:29:08.160 --> 01:29:12.590
So x0 from your standard
Gaussian distribution,

01:29:12.590 --> 01:29:15.990
your clean image, so
x1 from your training

01:29:15.990 --> 01:29:21.270
set, which is pdata, and you
also sample your time step.

01:29:21.270 --> 01:29:26.150
And what you do is you
construct xt, your noise image.

01:29:26.150 --> 01:29:30.310
And what I'm telling you is
thanks to the loss function

01:29:30.310 --> 01:29:38.150
that we derived, what we do is
we use xt, the noise image and t

01:29:38.150 --> 01:29:42.550
to predict x1 minus x0, which
is the conditional vector

01:29:42.550 --> 01:29:49.750
field via this model that
is your vector field model.

01:29:49.750 --> 01:29:54.230
And you're going to compute
the loss and backpropagate.

01:29:54.230 --> 01:29:55.770
That's how you do training.

01:29:55.770 --> 01:29:57.370
Now, how do you do inference?

01:29:57.370 --> 01:30:02.570
Well, inference, you just sample
from an easy data distribution.

01:30:02.570 --> 01:30:06.750
And here it's, again,
standard, normal distribution.

01:30:06.750 --> 01:30:12.950
And what you're going to do is
to numerically solve the ODE.

01:30:12.950 --> 01:30:16.530
So what you do is you go
time step to time step,

01:30:16.530 --> 01:30:19.710
and you use your
learned vector fields,

01:30:19.710 --> 01:30:26.310
and you end up with your
final estimated image x1.

01:30:26.310 --> 01:30:28.510
And that's how you do inference.

01:30:28.510 --> 01:30:32.830
And with that, I'm going
to give it to Shervine.

01:30:32.830 --> 01:30:34.710
Thank you, Afshine.

01:30:34.710 --> 01:30:37.910
So now we're going to see
in a more visual manner

01:30:37.910 --> 01:30:41.390
the training process and
discuss some edge cases

01:30:41.390 --> 01:30:44.230
and how to solve
them in practice.

01:30:44.230 --> 01:30:46.110
So you remember the
training process

01:30:46.110 --> 01:30:49.030
that Afshine just mentioned.

01:30:49.030 --> 01:30:52.410
You sample x0 from the
normal distribution,

01:30:52.410 --> 01:30:55.470
you sample x1 from
your training set.

01:30:55.470 --> 01:30:59.750
And what you do is that you draw
a straight line between the two,

01:30:59.750 --> 01:31:06.120
and you try to fit the
velocity to this x1 minus x0.

01:31:06.120 --> 01:31:11.280
Now I want you to imagine that
we do that for a given pair.

01:31:11.280 --> 01:31:14.360
And now for another pair.

01:31:14.360 --> 01:31:19.960
And suppose that these pairings
intersect at a given point.

01:31:19.960 --> 01:31:25.120
And let's suppose that this
intersection is for a same t.

01:31:25.120 --> 01:31:30.480
So in other words, we're trying
to fit the loss at a same x,

01:31:30.480 --> 01:31:35.040
at a same T on two
different vectors.

01:31:35.040 --> 01:31:39.660
So who knows what the resulting
learned velocity will be?

01:31:44.120 --> 01:31:45.740
So it cannot be two
velocities right.

01:31:45.740 --> 01:31:48.180
We cannot have two
vectors at a single point.

01:31:48.180 --> 01:31:51.360
It has to be one.

01:31:51.360 --> 01:31:57.680
So we have this MSE loss
that will make it such

01:31:57.680 --> 01:32:00.560
that it will be an
average of all the vectors

01:32:00.560 --> 01:32:03.720
that we tried to fit at
that given point points x

01:32:03.720 --> 01:32:10.520
and at given point t and you
see that as being a problem

01:32:10.520 --> 01:32:14.840
or, so what does that mean?

01:32:14.840 --> 01:32:18.480
So here in practice, let's
say you're at inference time

01:32:18.480 --> 01:32:22.880
and you want to go from,
let's say, x0 to x1.

01:32:22.880 --> 01:32:27.360
And let's say you have learned
a vector fields of velocity that

01:32:27.360 --> 01:32:28.660
doesn't go to x1.

01:32:28.660 --> 01:32:33.560
That goes in an
average direction.

01:32:33.560 --> 01:32:38.480
Are you going to be
able to go to x1?

01:32:38.480 --> 01:32:41.160
No, right?

01:32:41.160 --> 01:32:43.960
So the thing that will
occur is that you're

01:32:43.960 --> 01:32:48.040
going to have something
averaged that might repel

01:32:48.040 --> 01:32:54.600
the trajectories that you try to
learn into different directions.

01:32:54.600 --> 01:32:58.760
But be reassured,
we showed that these

01:32:58.760 --> 01:33:01.600
learned vector fields
was such that if you

01:33:01.600 --> 01:33:04.800
sample a point in the
normal distribution

01:33:04.800 --> 01:33:06.820
and follow the
learned vector field,

01:33:06.820 --> 01:33:11.120
you land into a
distribution land, that will

01:33:11.120 --> 01:33:12.780
be your distribution pdata.

01:33:12.780 --> 01:33:14.360
So that part is fine.

01:33:14.360 --> 01:33:18.080
But still, you
have this property

01:33:18.080 --> 01:33:21.400
that the learned
trajectories are not

01:33:21.400 --> 01:33:25.760
going to be the same as the
ones that you teach your model.

01:33:25.760 --> 01:33:30.160
And that is a source of what
I would call complexity, where

01:33:30.160 --> 01:33:34.160
you teach a model
that doesn't follow

01:33:34.160 --> 01:33:36.180
what you said at test time.

01:33:36.180 --> 01:33:37.960
So that is inherently--

01:33:37.960 --> 01:33:44.960
so your intuition could make you
feel that this is not efficient.

01:33:44.960 --> 01:33:47.560
And the second thing
that I want to say

01:33:47.560 --> 01:33:51.520
is that you could have
this phenomenon happening,

01:33:51.520 --> 01:33:56.320
even though the
points of intersection

01:33:56.320 --> 01:33:59.810
are not at the exact same T,
because in practice, you're

01:33:59.810 --> 01:34:03.290
going to learn this vector
field with a neural network.

01:34:03.290 --> 01:34:06.450
And neural network
are going to be

01:34:06.450 --> 01:34:10.010
a class of functions that are
going to be smoothing things

01:34:10.010 --> 01:34:11.130
in space.

01:34:11.130 --> 01:34:15.650
So you won't have a
drastic change of velocity

01:34:15.650 --> 01:34:18.210
for neighboring t's.

01:34:18.210 --> 01:34:22.690
And so because
one thing that you

01:34:22.690 --> 01:34:25.050
could have told me
is, but Shervine,

01:34:25.050 --> 01:34:26.530
that is never going to happen.

01:34:26.530 --> 01:34:30.590
01 is an interval with
infinitely many numbers.

01:34:30.590 --> 01:34:33.410
You're never going to draw the
same t, and you would be right.

01:34:33.410 --> 01:34:36.970
And I'm just saying that it
could be valid for neighboring

01:34:36.970 --> 01:34:38.730
t's.

01:34:38.730 --> 01:34:47.490
And more importantly, let's say
you sample your point x0 out

01:34:47.490 --> 01:34:49.490
of your normal distribution.

01:34:49.490 --> 01:34:51.510
What is going to
be your issue here?

01:34:51.510 --> 01:34:54.410
Afshine mentioned that
you want to integrate

01:34:54.410 --> 01:34:56.650
the ODE at inference time.

01:34:56.650 --> 01:34:59.170
And we had seen
last time that you

01:34:59.170 --> 01:35:04.570
want to find methods that
optimize that number of NFEs

01:35:04.570 --> 01:35:07.370
in such a way that you can
find your target sample

01:35:07.370 --> 01:35:09.810
in a minimum amount of steps.

01:35:09.810 --> 01:35:13.410
And when you have a path
that is getting curved,

01:35:13.410 --> 01:35:16.970
so you don't have these straight
lines at inference time,

01:35:16.970 --> 01:35:20.670
you're not going to be able to
solve that in a single step.

01:35:20.670 --> 01:35:22.650
Because let's say you use Euler.

01:35:22.650 --> 01:35:27.170
Euler is estimating the velocity
at the beginning points.

01:35:27.170 --> 01:35:29.590
And if you try to do
it in a single step,

01:35:29.590 --> 01:35:31.790
it will end up somewhere else.

01:35:31.790 --> 01:35:35.450
It will follow a straight
path, whereas the curve isn't

01:35:35.450 --> 01:35:36.950
going to be taken into account.

01:35:39.570 --> 01:35:42.010
And you're going to tell
me, but Shervine, you

01:35:42.010 --> 01:35:44.110
have tricks in your pockets.

01:35:44.110 --> 01:35:47.690
Last time you talked
about a DPM solver

01:35:47.690 --> 01:35:51.010
in a different setting that
was able to reframe things

01:35:51.010 --> 01:35:52.290
in an exact way.

01:35:52.290 --> 01:35:53.570
Well, no.

01:35:53.570 --> 01:35:56.850
This time, the ODE that
we want to integrate

01:35:56.850 --> 01:35:58.630
has no linear components.

01:35:58.630 --> 01:36:01.770
It's all non-linear
because here we're directly

01:36:01.770 --> 01:36:04.970
learning that velocity field.

01:36:04.970 --> 01:36:07.970
So no DPM solver here.

01:36:07.970 --> 01:36:11.750
You need to use traditional
sampler techniques.

01:36:15.330 --> 01:36:17.350
So not quite happy.

01:36:17.350 --> 01:36:19.570
We're happy but not quite.

01:36:19.570 --> 01:36:23.010
So now I'm going to walk
you through the intuition

01:36:23.010 --> 01:36:30.410
of a retraining or fine tuning
procedure that is called reflow.

01:36:30.410 --> 01:36:34.210
And so first I'm going to
recap the training procedure

01:36:34.210 --> 01:36:35.810
that we have already.

01:36:35.810 --> 01:36:39.410
So you sample a point x0,
you sample a point x1,

01:36:39.410 --> 01:36:43.890
you draw a path and then
you fit a velocity on it.

01:36:43.890 --> 01:36:49.210
So it is called in that paper
a one rectified flow model.

01:36:49.210 --> 01:36:55.940
And you obtain trajectories that
we saw we're going to be curved.

01:36:55.940 --> 01:37:01.740
And one idea that we have
here, one temptation,

01:37:01.740 --> 01:37:04.420
is to locate these new pairings.

01:37:04.420 --> 01:37:06.480
So you started from point x0.

01:37:06.480 --> 01:37:07.920
You integrated the ODE.

01:37:07.920 --> 01:37:13.140
You ended up at somewhere in
the distribution of pdata.

01:37:13.140 --> 01:37:18.100
You're tempted to use these
new pairs as your new pairings,

01:37:18.100 --> 01:37:22.060
because they will
be, by construction,

01:37:22.060 --> 01:37:26.260
in such a way that the
velocity field will carry

01:37:26.260 --> 01:37:30.360
the trajectory directly there.

01:37:30.360 --> 01:37:32.920
So the reflow
procedure says, OK,

01:37:32.920 --> 01:37:36.140
let's take these
new pairs as those

01:37:36.140 --> 01:37:40.980
that we use to
retrain the model.

01:37:40.980 --> 01:37:46.140
So this will be our new lines
that we fit our new model.

01:37:46.140 --> 01:37:49.380
And so this is the reflow step.

01:37:49.380 --> 01:37:52.220
And you do that
process again and again

01:37:52.220 --> 01:37:56.420
until your lines
becomes straighter.

01:37:56.420 --> 01:37:59.040
And once that is the
case, at inference time,

01:37:59.040 --> 01:38:01.980
you're able to use a limited
amount of inference steps

01:38:01.980 --> 01:38:09.500
in order to get your
sample in the pdata space.

01:38:09.500 --> 01:38:11.440
So does this
procedure make sense?

01:38:11.440 --> 01:38:11.940
Yep?

01:38:21.820 --> 01:38:25.700
So the comment is
if you retrain, then

01:38:25.700 --> 01:38:28.640
the two points are going to
be from the new distribution.

01:38:28.640 --> 01:38:30.780
So the magic in all of this--

01:38:30.780 --> 01:38:33.860
so first, you're going to keep
that Gaussian distribution

01:38:33.860 --> 01:38:34.760
at the beginning.

01:38:34.760 --> 01:38:36.260
That will not change.

01:38:36.260 --> 01:38:38.540
And at the end,
we're going to see

01:38:38.540 --> 01:38:40.780
that there are some
properties that

01:38:40.780 --> 01:38:44.900
makes it such that the resulting
points that you get still

01:38:44.900 --> 01:38:47.580
preserve the
distribution p data.

01:38:47.580 --> 01:38:51.110
So there is some
theoretical justification

01:38:51.110 --> 01:38:53.910
that it does follow pdata.

01:38:53.910 --> 01:38:55.955
But this is a great point.

01:38:55.955 --> 01:38:58.330
We're going to discuss that
in more detail in two slides.

01:39:01.750 --> 01:39:05.870
Any other questions or comments?

01:39:05.870 --> 01:39:08.790
OK, so you're right
to be skeptical.

01:39:08.790 --> 01:39:11.410
So this works because
of two things.

01:39:11.410 --> 01:39:15.150
So first, actually, one slide.

01:39:15.150 --> 01:39:20.550
So we need to show that this
follows the distribution pdata.

01:39:20.550 --> 01:39:23.390
So I'm not going to give
you the whole proof, just

01:39:23.390 --> 01:39:24.610
the gist of it.

01:39:24.610 --> 01:39:29.870
So you start by defining
this random variable that

01:39:29.870 --> 01:39:32.430
starts at x0.

01:39:32.430 --> 01:39:36.350
And you integrate the ODE
to end up in the new space

01:39:36.350 --> 01:39:46.590
and define this velocity for the
x random variable and the proof

01:39:46.590 --> 01:39:50.990
to show that the law that you
get for the end distribution

01:39:50.990 --> 01:39:53.370
after a reflow
procedure goes through,

01:39:53.370 --> 01:40:00.790
showing that these two
probability flows respect

01:40:00.790 --> 01:40:03.430
the same continuity equation.

01:40:03.430 --> 01:40:08.390
And with some uniqueness
theorem and the presence

01:40:08.390 --> 01:40:14.950
of initial conditions at the
fact that dt equals 0 is still

01:40:14.950 --> 01:40:17.790
the Gaussian
distribution, you can

01:40:17.790 --> 01:40:22.550
show that it's valid for all t
and, in particular, t equals 1.

01:40:22.550 --> 01:40:26.090
So yeah, just hand-waving
the proof here.

01:40:26.090 --> 01:40:29.830
So it's theoretically the case.

01:40:29.830 --> 01:40:32.150
But you are right
to be skeptical,

01:40:32.150 --> 01:40:35.030
because once you
integrate the ODE,

01:40:35.030 --> 01:40:39.250
you have errors that
come in from two sources.

01:40:39.250 --> 01:40:42.410
So the first error is
the discretization error.

01:40:42.410 --> 01:40:46.890
So as we said, we're
sampling these new points.

01:40:46.890 --> 01:40:50.050
So maybe you have some errors
in the way you do these steps.

01:40:50.050 --> 01:40:54.070
So you don't necessarily end
up in a theoretical location

01:40:54.070 --> 01:40:55.750
you should be at.

01:40:55.750 --> 01:40:59.550
And the second point is
that you're operating off

01:40:59.550 --> 01:41:02.830
of a learned vector field
that is approximated

01:41:02.830 --> 01:41:05.790
by a neural network,
and it is possible

01:41:05.790 --> 01:41:07.770
that you haven't
learned it perfectly.

01:41:07.770 --> 01:41:09.990
I mean, of course
it's sure that you

01:41:09.990 --> 01:41:12.510
haven't learned it perfectly.

01:41:12.510 --> 01:41:16.430
And the quality of
your results will

01:41:16.430 --> 01:41:20.310
be a function of how well
you learn that vector field

01:41:20.310 --> 01:41:22.630
and of your
discretization errors.

01:41:22.630 --> 01:41:24.870
And we're going to see
very soon that this

01:41:24.870 --> 01:41:30.070
is one of the reasons why we
don't perform this procedure too

01:41:30.070 --> 01:41:34.430
many times, because the
degradation is going to be

01:41:34.430 --> 01:41:38.230
a trade off to balance here.

01:41:38.230 --> 01:41:40.490
So this is the first
point I wanted to justify.

01:41:40.490 --> 01:41:42.550
The second point I
wanted to justify

01:41:42.550 --> 01:41:47.600
is we hand waved the fact that
the flows like the trajectories

01:41:47.600 --> 01:41:51.360
were getting
straighter, and there

01:41:51.360 --> 01:41:55.082
is a mathematical way of
writing it down and proving it.

01:41:55.082 --> 01:41:57.040
And this is also something
that is being proven

01:41:57.040 --> 01:41:58.800
in case you're interested.

01:41:58.800 --> 01:42:01.540
And for the interest of time,
I'm not going to be covering it,

01:42:01.540 --> 01:42:11.160
but that s quantity quantifies
straightness and compares

01:42:11.160 --> 01:42:14.860
the actual velocity with
the theoretical one.

01:42:14.860 --> 01:42:19.060
And we show that it
decays with a good speed.

01:42:19.060 --> 01:42:24.120
And this is why we're saying
it's getting straighter.

01:42:24.120 --> 01:42:27.160
And exactly as we discussed,
the reflow procedure

01:42:27.160 --> 01:42:30.880
is one that you wouldn't
want to do too many times,

01:42:30.880 --> 01:42:34.640
but usually, one procedure
is already great.

01:42:34.640 --> 01:42:39.380
And the goal is for us to use a
very simple sampling techniques,

01:42:39.380 --> 01:42:42.960
such as Euler, optimally
in just a few steps,

01:42:42.960 --> 01:42:45.400
finding the right solution.

01:42:45.400 --> 01:42:47.780
And of course, it
isn't a free lunch.

01:42:47.780 --> 01:42:49.400
It comes at a trade off.

01:42:49.400 --> 01:42:56.720
So you're trading off simplicity
and potentially lower quality

01:42:56.720 --> 01:43:01.840
for faster inference
time speeds.

01:43:01.840 --> 01:43:03.000
OK, great.

01:43:03.000 --> 01:43:06.240
So this past three lectures,
including this one,

01:43:06.240 --> 01:43:12.280
we've come across these concepts
of diffusion, score matching,

01:43:12.280 --> 01:43:14.840
and now flows, flow matching.

01:43:14.840 --> 01:43:17.560
And I want to spend
five minutes together

01:43:17.560 --> 01:43:21.680
to tie all of these frameworks
together in a way that

01:43:21.680 --> 01:43:25.600
will hopefully show links
between them and the fact

01:43:25.600 --> 01:43:29.700
that we are solving the
same category of problems

01:43:29.700 --> 01:43:32.760
but from different lenses.

01:43:32.760 --> 01:43:34.400
And in order to
do that, I'm going

01:43:34.400 --> 01:43:38.560
to look at each of the
processes that we defined

01:43:38.560 --> 01:43:42.680
and then look at the difference
with respect to each method.

01:43:42.680 --> 01:43:46.900
So the first one is
the forward process.

01:43:46.900 --> 01:43:48.200
So it's a long time ago.

01:43:48.200 --> 01:43:52.240
Already two weeks ago, we talked
about DPM and all this class

01:43:52.240 --> 01:43:56.320
of discrete time, diffusion.

01:43:56.320 --> 01:44:00.960
And then we saw in score-based
diffusion world that if you

01:44:00.960 --> 01:44:07.800
reframed these equations and
converted it into continuous

01:44:07.800 --> 01:44:12.760
time, put some delta t to 0, you
could get some SDE formulation

01:44:12.760 --> 01:44:20.680
that was equivalent to it, Where
we saw at the DPM solver section

01:44:20.680 --> 01:44:24.840
that actually, in diffusion
space, you could approximate--

01:44:24.840 --> 01:44:28.360
I mean, you could always
assume that f of x and t

01:44:28.360 --> 01:44:30.640
was linear in x.

01:44:30.640 --> 01:44:31.780
And this is why.

01:44:31.780 --> 01:44:33.780
So it's something that
we haven't seen so far

01:44:33.780 --> 01:44:36.400
but I'm telling you that
you have a nice closed form

01:44:36.400 --> 01:44:41.770
expression with respect
to f and g of xt.

01:44:41.770 --> 01:44:45.930
And now we saw today
that you can write down

01:44:45.930 --> 01:44:51.850
xt as an interpolation between
x0 and x1 in flow matching.

01:44:51.850 --> 01:44:56.690
And now we're going to see
that in all of these methods,

01:44:56.690 --> 01:44:58.710
you start from a clean sample.

01:44:58.710 --> 01:45:03.930
So in a diffusion space, we
start the clean sample from t

01:45:03.930 --> 01:45:05.250
equals 0.

01:45:05.250 --> 01:45:07.090
But in flow matching
space, we saw

01:45:07.090 --> 01:45:09.450
that the notation was reversed.

01:45:09.450 --> 01:45:12.130
Clean sample is t equals 1.

01:45:12.130 --> 01:45:14.130
And on the other
side of the spectrum,

01:45:14.130 --> 01:45:19.910
you have noise, which was in
diffusion world t equals big t.

01:45:19.910 --> 01:45:22.370
And here it's t equals 0.

01:45:22.370 --> 01:45:26.530
So you're here drawing an
interpolation between the two

01:45:26.530 --> 01:45:29.050
in each of these cases.

01:45:29.050 --> 01:45:32.410
Now when we look at
the loss functions,

01:45:32.410 --> 01:45:38.370
we saw that the diffusion
view of writing things down

01:45:38.370 --> 01:45:40.670
was focusing on noise.

01:45:44.010 --> 01:45:47.390
The score matching view
was linking scores,

01:45:47.390 --> 01:45:51.450
and you had a nice relationship
between noise and score.

01:45:51.450 --> 01:45:55.450
And in the flow matching world,
you were directly fitting

01:45:55.450 --> 01:45:58.410
the velocity field such
that you have the simplest

01:45:58.410 --> 01:46:02.450
ODE to integrate in the end.

01:46:02.450 --> 01:46:04.810
Now, when we look at
the generation process,

01:46:04.810 --> 01:46:08.050
we saw in the diffusion
world that you

01:46:08.050 --> 01:46:11.530
had all these
steps to go through

01:46:11.530 --> 01:46:14.370
with some stochasticity,
which you also

01:46:14.370 --> 01:46:18.290
had in the score-based
diffusion world,

01:46:18.290 --> 01:46:23.370
and you had for the first two,
a deterministic counterpart.

01:46:23.370 --> 01:46:25.530
And you will notice
that the flow matching

01:46:25.530 --> 01:46:28.490
one doesn't have such
a balance because it's

01:46:28.490 --> 01:46:31.810
deterministic to start with.

01:46:31.810 --> 01:46:36.740
And I'm just showing you some
relationships between all

01:46:36.740 --> 01:46:38.220
these methods, but
actually, there

01:46:38.220 --> 01:46:42.940
is a whole theory linking these
concepts in a very nice way.

01:46:42.940 --> 01:46:46.680
And this is why I recommend
if you're interested.

01:46:46.680 --> 01:46:48.640
So it's out of scope
for this class.

01:46:48.640 --> 01:46:52.660
And just if you're interested,
to take a look at the paper

01:46:52.660 --> 01:46:58.380
called Stochastic Interpolants
that writes down equations that

01:46:58.380 --> 01:47:00.260
links all of these together.

01:47:00.260 --> 01:47:06.160
And turns out that r of
noise, score, and velocity,

01:47:06.160 --> 01:47:09.380
if you know two of them, you
can deduce the third one.

01:47:09.380 --> 01:47:13.540
And that paper designs
a problem space

01:47:13.540 --> 01:47:17.660
that is bigger than what each
of these frameworks detailed.

01:47:17.660 --> 01:47:20.580
And all of these
frameworks can live

01:47:20.580 --> 01:47:25.260
in the same unified framework.

01:47:25.260 --> 01:47:28.890
And with that, I hope
you have a great weekend.

